  "worked_solution": "We start by solving $2x\\le 8:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & \\le 8 \\\\ x & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/f3c81896babacce5e34f1cbb9f179f6843ae46fa45b8b4399b8f7f8eb11284c8.png)\n\nThen, we solve $3x+4\\ge 7:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+4 & \\ge 7 \\\\ 3x & \\ge 3 \\\\ x & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/c9f85fc4864f321722a7cb0b017eab1979aa6a84cd07d08b150b625a1720f25a.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196049/assets/9c045011d942bd93712aa87307994f00d9be9613c6dd547476f4fe7bd1622e19.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$",
